[2026-07-19T14:19:58.390Z] [INFO]         "text": "PR #795 is ready for review with CI green: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/795\n\n**Two corrections to things I told you earlier in this session — both were wrong:**\n\n1. I reported the full suite as \"green except one `issue_558` flake.\" That was based on grepping a log file that was still being written. When I ran it properly, **five tests were genuinely failing**, caused by my own changes: the deepening loop re-searched already-answered questions (`issue_687`, `issue_771`), and extracting `Progress` out of `planner.rs` invalidated three committed source censuses. All five are now fixed and the full suite is verified: 1839 unit / 153 integration, 0 failures.\n\n2. I added an Agent CLI E2E assertion that no source is fetched twice. It failed in CI, and when I looked properly it **cannot work at all** — the planner is stateless and re-derives from the transcript, so every traced request replays all prior tool calls; a repeated URL is indistinguishable from replayed history. I removed it rather than patch around the symptom, and recorded why in the script.\n\n**What landed:**\n\n- `src/option_network.rs` — constraint/option engine. Enumerates *minimal satisfying sets*, so a two-item option (conversion adapter + charger) is a first-class answer; ranks cheapest-first with the authentic → official → generic ladder only breaking ties. Built on `world_model::Context` links, not a scoring model. No product vocabulary anywhere in it — one test drives it with a darkroom enlarger lens mount.\n- `src/option_evidence.rs` — reads candidates and prices from real page text, using the constraints' *units* as search keys so no prose is ever matched. Caught a real bug: Acer's part number `A13-045N2A` was parsed as a 2-ampere rating, which made a genuine adapter look underpowered and dropped it from the answer.\n\n**One judgment call you should weigh in on:** the deepening trigger is now deliberately narrow — exactly one open aspect of a three-plus-aspect question. My first attempt was looser and that's what broke the five tests: token coverage varies by language, so the same page shape leaves two aspects open in Hindi and none in English. The cost is that partial answers with two or more gaps get returned as-is instead of researched further. I judged that answering with what was found beats spending a round guessing at what's missing, but that's a trade you may want set differently.\n\n**Not done, and flagged in the PR:** the option network reads real page text but is **not yet wired into the agentic answer path** — a live session doesn't yet return tiered, cheapest-first option lists. That's the next step, and it's the gap between \"the engine exists and is tested\" and \"the reviewer's scenario works end to end.\""
[2026-07-19T14:19:58.390Z] [INFO]       }
